One thing I would change in your Ansible role now: the task named:

```text
Wait for remaining nodes to be ready - no CNI
```

should not wait for Kubernetes `Ready=True` when `cni: none`. That condition cannot succeed before Cilium is installed.

Instead, that task should only verify that the nodes are registered:

```yaml
- name: Wait for remaining nodes to register - no CNI
  ansible.builtin.command:
    cmd: >
      /var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl
      --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml
      get node {{ item }}
  register: node_registered
  until: node_registered.rc == 0
  retries: 60
  delay: 10
  changed_when: false
  loop: "{{ groups['masters'] }}"
```

Then after Cilium installs, add the real readiness check:

```yaml
- name: Wait for control-plane nodes to become Ready
  ansible.builtin.command:
    cmd: >
      /var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl
      --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml
      wait
      --for=condition=Ready
      node/{{ item }}
      --timeout=600s
  loop: "{{ groups['masters'] }}"
```

Your cluster is now at the exact point where the **Cilium playbook should run**. The important thing is not to interpret `NotReady` as another RKE2 failure; with `cni: none`, it is the expected state until Cilium initializes networking.
















